 \"max_rtol\": 0.05, \"required_match_ratio\": 0.98}}\n{\"uuid\": \"67119fc1-69aa-53a7-acd5-dae7dbbcbd56\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"time_dim\": 1688, \"time_after_conv\": 211}, \"inputs\": {\"input_features\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d1_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d1_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d2_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d2_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d3_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d3_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_out_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"positional_embedding\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"embed_scale\": {\"type\": \"scalar\", \"value\": 32.0}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 1.1, \"max_rtol\": 0.05, \"required_match_ratio\": 0.98}}\n{\"uuid\": \"10361f03-c94e-514e-bb4a-6799525e65d5\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 32, \"time_dim\": 920, \"time_after_conv\": 115}, \"inputs\": {\"input_features\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d1_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d1_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d2_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d2_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d3_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv2d3_bias\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"conv_out_weight\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"positional_embedding\": {\"type\": \"random\"}, \"embed_scale\": {\"type\": \"scalar\", \"value\": 32.0}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 1.1, \"max_rtol\": 0.05, \"required_match_ratio\": 0.98}}\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DHWqE3LV5h7TUR59NaGf28","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/015 Audio Sinusoidal Position Embedding with Conv Projection\n2\t\n3\tTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (sm_80, Ampere)** — A100-class. bf16 tensor cores via `mma.sync`\n4\t(ldmatrix), fp32 accumulation. **No** TMA / tcgen05 / CLC (those are Hopper/Blackwell). The\n5\t`KernelWiki` skill is Blackwell/Hopper-specific, so its concrete PRs are mostly *not*\n6\ttransferable here; `ncu-report-skill` requires a profiler run, which is **forbidden** by the\n7\ttask rules. So this task is driven by first-principles reasoning + the official feedback\n8\tevaluator.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json` reference)\n13\t\n14\t`run(input_features, conv2d1_w, conv2d1_b, conv2d2_w, conv2d2_b, conv2d3_w, conv2d3_b,\n15\tconv_out_weight, positional_embedding, embed_scale)`:\n16\t\n17\t1. **Conv1**: `F.conv2d(input_features[b,1,80,Tin], w1[384,1,3,3], b1, stride=2, pad=1)` →\n18\t   `[b,384,40,T1]`; then `F.gelu`.\n19\t2. **Conv2**: `F.conv2d(x, w2[384,384,3,3], b2, stride=2, pad=1)` → `[b,384,20,T2]`; `F.gelu`.\n20\t3. **Conv3**: `F.conv2d(x, w3[384,384,3,3], b3, stride=2, pad=1)` → `[b,384,10,T3]`; `F.gelu`.\n21\t4. **Reshape**: `permute(0,3,1,2).contiguous().view(b, T3, 384*10=3840)`.\n22\t   Flatten order is **channel-outer, freq-inner**: column `k = c*10 + f`.\n23\t5. **Linear (no bias)**: `x[b,T3,3840] @ conv_out_weight[1024,3840]^T` → `[b,T3,1024]`.\n24\t6. **Scale**: `x * embed_scale` (`embed_scale = sqrt(1024) = 32.0` in all feedback workloads).\n25\t7. **Add PE**: `x + positional_embedding[:T3].unsqueeze(0)` (broadcast over batch; indexed by\n26\t   time-after-conv `t`).\n27\t\n28\tOutput: `hidden_states[b, T3, 1024]`, **bf16**.\n29\t\n30\tAll conv/gelu/linear are executed in **bf16** in the reference (inputs are bf16); PyTorch/cuDNN\n31\taccumulate internally in fp32 but **round each intermediate activation to bf16**. This\n32\t\"where does rounding happen\" detail governs numerical matching (see §4).\n33\t\n34\t### Constants\n35\t`d_model=1024`, `num_mel_bins=80`, `max_source_positions=1500`, `downsample_hidden=384`,\n36\t`freq_after_conv=10`, `conv_out_dim=3840`, `kernel=3`, `stride=2`, `pad=1`.\n37\tGELU here is `F.gelu` **default = exact erf** GELU: `0.5*x*(1+erf(x/√2))` (NOT tanh approx).\n38\t\n39\t### Verified per-workload shapes  (Hout=⌊(Hin−1)/2⌋+1; freq always 40→20→10)\n40\t\n41\t| wl | b  | Tin  | T1   | T2  | T3  | ref T3 | M_lin=b·T3 |\n42\t|----|----|------|------|-----|-----|--------|-----------|\n43\t| w1 | 8  | 3256 | 1628 | 814 | 407 | 407 ✓  | 3256 |\n44\t| w2 | 1  | 3000 | 1500 | 750 | 375 | 375 ✓  |  375 |\n45\t| w3 | 16 | 2048 | 1024 | 512 | 256 | 256 ✓  | 4096 |\n46\t| w4 | 2  | 1688 | 844  | 422 | 211 | 211 ✓  |  422 |\n47\t| w5 | 32 | 920  | 460  | 230 | 115 | 115 ✓  | 3680 |\n48\t\n49\tTolerances: `max_atol` 1.1 (w3: 1.3), `max_rtol` 0.05, `required_match_ratio` 0.98 — **loose**\n50\t(bf16 output scaled by 32 + PE ∈ [−1,1]).\n51\t\n52\t---\n53\t\n54\t## 2. Cost / roofline (hand-computed, A800 ≈ A100: ~312 bf16 TFLOP/s, ~2 TB/s)\n55\t\n56\tFLOPs = 2·M_out·(Cin·9) with M_out=b·Hout·Wout; conv1 has Cin=1 (K=9).\n57\t\n58\t| stage | w3 (largest) FLOPs | note |\n59\t|-------|--------------------|------|\n60\t| conv1 | ~4.5 GFLOP  | Cin=1, K=9 → memory/launch bound, NOT tensor-core efficient |\n61\t| **conv2** | **~435 GFLOP** | dominant; K=3456, tensor-core GEMM |\n62\t| conv3 | ~109 GFLOP  | K=3456, tensor-core GEMM |\n63\t| linear| ~32 GFLOP   | M=4096,K=3840,N=1024 GEMM |\n64\t\n65\t**Compute is dominated by conv2, then conv3.** Ideal conv2 ≈ 1.4 ms; realistic 2–4 ms.\n66\t\n67\t**Memory**: intermediate1 `[b,384,40,T1]` bf16 is *large*: w3 = 16·384·40·1024·2 ≈ **503 MB**\n68\t(largest across wls; w1 400 MB, w5 452 MB). conv2 re-reads it (9 taps, with L2 reuse ≈ once).\n69\tintermediate2 `[b,384,20,T2]` ≈ 126 MB (w3); intermediate3 ≈ 32 MB. So conv1 write +\n70\tconv2 read of intermediate1 is the main bandwidth term (~0.5–1 GB traffic).\n71\t\n72\t### Why beating the reference is plausible\n73\tThe reference launches ~10+ kernels: 3×(conv + gelu) = 6, a `permute→contiguous` copy (~1),\n74\tlinear, `mul`, `add`. Our fused design uses **4 kernels** and removes the 32 MB\n75\tpermute-contiguous copy + several elementwise passes. cuDNN also handles the awkward\n76\t**tiny freq dim** (Hout=20/10) which can push it to suboptimal algos/tiles; a purpose-built\n77\timplicit-GEMM with GELU/scale/PE fused into epilogues has real headroom even if per-conv we\n78\tonly match cuDNN.\n79\t\n80\t---\n81\t\n82\t## 3. Triton design space\n83\t\n84\t### 3.1 Chosen pipeline (baseline candidate c001): 4 kernels, implicit-GEMM convs\n85\t\n86\t- **K-A `conv1+gelu`**: input `[b,1,80,Tin]` → intermediate1 stored **NHWC** `[b,40,T1,384]`.\n87\t  Cin=1 so K=9; direct kernel: each program computes a tile of output pixels × BLOCK_N out\n88\t  channels, gathers the 3×3 single-channel window (masked for pad), MAC over 9 taps\n89\t  (pad K→16 for a `tl.dot`, or explicit FMA since Cout vectorizes well). Add bias, GELU,\n90\t  round bf16.\n91\t- **K-B `conv2+gelu`**: reads intermediate1 NHWC → intermediate2 NHWC `[b,20,T2,384]`.\n92\t  Implicit GEMM: M=b·20·T2 output pixels, N=Cout=384, K=Cin·9=3456. Loop over the 9 taps;\n93\t  each tap = `tl.dot(A[BLOCK_M, 384], W_tap[384, BLOCK_N])` fp32-accum. NHWC makes the A-block\n94\t  read **contiguous along Cin** (coalesced). Bias + GELU + bf16 in epilogue.\n95\t- **K-C `conv3+gelu` (fused reshape)**: reads intermediate2 NHWC → writes **directly into the\n96\t  linear A-matrix** `[b, T3, 3840]` at column `cout*10 + f` (channel-outer/freq-inner). This\n97\t  *is* the reference `permute(0,3,1,2).view(...)`, so the permute-contiguous copy disappears.\n98\t  Same implicit-GEMM structure as K-B (M=b·10·T3).\n99\t- **K-D `linear + scale + PE`**: GEMM `A[M=b·T3, K=3840] @ Wt[3840,1024]` with epilogue\n100\t  `acc*embed_scale + positional_embedding[t]`, store bf16. A rows are contiguous in K\n101\t  (coalesced); PE row selected by time index `t` (same across batch).\n102\t\n103\t**Layout decision**: store all intermediates **NHWC** (channels-last, contiguous C) so every\n104\timplicit-GEMM A-block load is coalesced. We *own* the intermediate buffers, so we pick layout\n105\tfreely. The harness input is NCHW but Cin=1, so layout is irrelevant for conv1's input.\n106\t\n107\t**Weight layout for `tl.dot`**: convs need `W_tap[Cin, Cout]` = `weight[cout, cin, kh, kw]`\n108\t(strided in the given `[Cout,Cin,3,3]`). Weight is tiny (2.65 MB, L2-resident and reused).\n109\t- Option A (pure Triton, preferred for correctness-first): load with computed strides inside\n110\t  the kernel — no torch compute, just strided global loads.\n111\t- Option B (later opt): pre-permute weights to `[9,Cin,Cout]` contiguous. A `.permute().\n112\t  contiguous()` is a **layout-only** transform (not the conv math) — arguably \"launch\n113\t  plumbing,\" but it borders the \"no torch computation\" rule, so if used it will be done via a\n114\t  small Triton copy kernel to stay unambiguous.\n115\t\n116\t### 3.2 Alternatives / later candidates\n117\t- **im2col + cuBLAS/`tl.dot` GEMM**: rejected — materialized im2col for conv2 is ~0.9 GB\n118\t  (M·3456), worse than implicit gather.\n119\t- **Fuse conv1 into conv2** (skip 0.5 GB intermediate1): conv2 pixel needs a 3×3 window of\n120\t  conv1 outputs, each conv1 output a 3×3 input window → up to 9× recompute of conv1. conv1 is\n121\t  cheap (K=9), so trading 0.5 GB traffic for recompute may win. Candidate for a later variant.\n122\t- **Fuse conv3 into linear**: linear row needs *all* 3840 K = all (c,f) of that time column of\n123\t  conv3 → would recompute conv3 fully per N-tile; likely not worth it. Keep separate.\n124\t- **Tap-blocking & tiling**: tune BLOCK_M∈{64,128}, BLOCK_N∈{64,128}, num_warps∈{4,8},\n125\t  num_stages∈{2,3,4}. Small freq means M is dominated by time; tile M over flattened\n126\t  (b,ho,wo). Boundary/pad handled by masking hi,wi at tap load.\n127\t- **conv1 vectorization**: since it's memory-bound, may fuse gelu and prefer a plain\n128\t  elementwise/tiled kernel over a padded `tl.dot`.\n129\t\n130\t---\n131\t\n132\t## 4. Numerical risks & mitigations\n133\t\n134\t- **Match rounding sites**: reference rounds each conv output to bf16 *before* GELU and each\n135\t  intermediate to bf16. Mitigation: accumulate conv in **fp32**, round the pre-activation to\n136\t  bf16, compute GELU in fp32 from that bf16 value, round result to bf16 → mirrors the ref's\n137\t  bf16 intermediates and limits chained-error drift across the 3-conv cascade.\n138\t- **GELU flavor**: use **exact erf** (`tl.math.erf`/libdevice), not tanh approx, to match\n139\t  `F.gelu` default. tanh approx is likely within atol but erf is free-ish and safer.\n140\t- **Accumulation precision**: fp32 accumulators for all `tl.dot` (Ampere bf16 MMA accum is\n141\t  fp32 by default). Never accumulate in bf16.\n142\t- **embed_scale**: apply as fp32 (32.0). PE added as fp32 then rounded to bf16. Output scaled\n143\t  by 32 ⇒ absolute errors scale up, but `atol=1.1` (w3 1.3) already accounts for this.\n144\t- **Padding correctness**: stride-2, pad-1 → boundary output pixels read out-of-range hi/wi;\n145\t  mask to 0 (not the bias). Off-by-one here is the most likely correctness bug — assert Hout\n146\t  formula `⌊(Hin−1)/2⌋+1` in the wrapper and cover freq edges (Hout row 0 and last).\n147\t- **Flatten order**: `k = c*10 + f` (channel-outer). A swapped order silently passes shapes\n148\t  but fails values — pin this in K-C's write indexing and cross-check against the weight’s\n149\t  column semantics.\n150\t- **bf16 dynamic range**: activations after Kaiming/Xavier init are O(1); no overflow risk.\n151\t- **Tolerance headroom**: rtol 0.05, match_ratio 0.98 allow 2% outliers — comfortable for\n152\t  bf16, but the 3-conv chain compounds rounding; if a workload fails, first suspect\n153\t  rounding-site mismatch or pad masking, not raw precision.\n154\t\n155\t---\n156\t\n157\t## 5. Validation strategy\n158\t\n159\t- **No external harness allowed**: cannot run torch reference, CUDA, profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n160\t  or the evaluator directly. Correctness/speed come **only** from\n161\t  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <cid>` over the 5 fixed workloads (= 1 evaluation).\n162\t- **In-code guards** (cheap, allowed): shape/stride asserts in the Python wrapper; assert\n163\t  computed T1/T2/T3 equal `⌊(n−1)/2⌋+1`; assert output shape `[b,T3,1024]` bf16; assert\n164\t  `conv_out_dim=3840`, `d_model=1024`.\n165\t- **Correctness-first ordering**: c001 = simplest fully-Triton correct pipeline (in-kernel\n166\t  strided weight loads, modest fixed tiles, exact erf, bf16 rounding at ref sites). Only after\n167\t  it passes all 5 do we tune tiles / fuse conv1→conv2 / weight pre-pack, each as a new\n168\t  immutable candidate ID with its own evaluation.\n169\t- **Regression discipline**: record per-workload pass + latency + geomean in `candidates.jsonl`;\n170\t  keep changes small per candidate so a geomean regression is attributable.\n171\t- **Fallback rule**: a failing Triton kernel is invalid — fix the Triton, never substitute a\n172\t  torch/CPU/numpy path.\n173\t\n174\t### Success criteria\n175\tGeomean speedup > 1.0 vs reference with **all 5 workloads passing** correctness; iterate on\n176\tconv2/conv3 tiling (dominant cost) and the conv1→conv2 fusion for the memory-bound term.\n177\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef68dcaa-cfdb-437b-a141-fe330f865751","uuid":"07e1c38c-683a-4599-8fee-71dbd4f406d5","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:57:22.064Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-015_audio_sinusoidal_position_embedding_with_conv_projection/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/015 Audio Sinusoidal Position Embedding with Conv Projection\n\nTarget HW: NVIDIA **A800 (sm_80, Ampere)** — A100-class. bf16 tensor cores via `mma.sync`\n(ldmatrix), fp32 accumulation. **No** TMA / tcgen05 / CLC (those are Hopper/Blackwell). The\n`KernelWiki` skill is Blackwell/Hopper-specific, so its concrete PRs are mostly *not*\ntransferable here; `ncu-report-skill` requires a profiler run, which is **forbidden** by the\ntask rules. So this task is driven by first-principles reasoning + the official feedback\nevaluator.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation semantics (from `task/definition.json` reference)\n\n`run(input_features, conv2d1_w, conv2d1_b, conv2d2_w, conv2d2_b, conv2d3_w, conv2d3_b,\nconv_out_weight, positional_embedding, embed_scale)`:\n\n1. **Conv1**: `F.conv2d(input_features[b,1,80,Tin], w1[384,1,3,3], b1, stride=2, pad=1)` →\n   `[b,384,40,T1]`; then `F.gelu`.\n2. **Conv2**: `F.conv2d(x, w2[384,384,3,3], b2, stride=2, pad=1)` → `[b,384,20,T2]`; `F.gelu`.\n3. **Conv3**: `F.conv2d(x, w3[384,384,3,3], b3, stride=2, pad=1)` → `[b,384,10,T3]`; `F.gelu`.\n4. **Reshape**: `permute(0,3,1,2).contiguous().view(b, T3, 384*10=3840)`.\n   Flatten order is **channel-outer, freq-inner**: column `k = c*10 + f`.\n5. **Linear (no bias)**: `x[b,T3,3840] @ conv_out_weight[1024,3840]^T` → `[b,T3,1024]`.\n6. **Scale**: `x * embed_scale` (`embed_scale = sqrt(1024) = 32.0` in all feedback workloads).\n7. **Add PE**: `x + positional_embedding[:T3].unsqueeze(0)` (broadcast over batch; indexed by\n   time-after-conv `t`).\n\nOutput: `hidden_states[b, T3, 1024]`, **bf16**.\n\nAll conv/gelu/linear are executed in **bf16** in the reference (inputs are bf16); PyTorch/cuDNN\naccumulate internally in fp32 but **round each intermediate activation to bf16**. This\n\"where does rounding happen\" detail governs numerical matching (see §4).\n\n### Constants\n`d_model=1024`, `num_mel_bins=80`, `max_source_positions=1500`, `downsample_hidden=384`,\n`freq_after_conv=10`, `conv_out_dim=3840`, `kernel=3`, `stride=2`, `pad=1`.\nGELU here is `F.gelu` **default = exact erf** GELU: `0.5*x*(1+erf(x/√2))` (NOT tanh approx).\n\n### Verified per-workload shapes  (Hout=⌊(Hin−1)/2⌋+1; freq always 40→20→10)\n\n| wl | b  | Tin  | T1   | T2  | T3  | ref T3 | M_lin=b·T3 |\n|----|----|------|------|-----|-----|--------|-----------|\n| w1 | 8  | 3256 | 1628 | 814 | 407 | 407 ✓  | 3256 |\n| w2 | 1  | 3000 | 1500 | 750 | 375 | 375 ✓  |  375 |\n| w3 | 16 | 2048 | 1024 | 512 | 256 | 256 ✓  | 4096 |\n| w4 | 2  | 1688 | 844  | 422 | 211 | 211 ✓  |  422 |\n| w5 | 32 | 920  | 460  | 230 | 115 | 115 ✓  | 3680 |\n\nTolerances: `max_atol` 1.1 (w3: 1.3), `max_rtol` 0.05, `required_match_ratio` 0.98 — **loose**\n(bf16 output scaled by 32 + PE ∈ [−1,1]).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cost / roofline (hand-computed, A800 ≈ A100: ~312 bf16 TFLOP/s, ~2 TB/s)\n\nFLOPs = 2·M_out·(Cin·9) with M_out=b·Hout·Wout; conv1 has Cin=1 (K=9).\n\n| stage | w3 (largest) FLOPs | note |\n|-------|--------------------|------|\n| conv1 | ~4.5 GFLOP  | Cin=1, K=9 → memory/launch bound, NOT tensor-core efficient |\n| **conv2** | **~435 GFLOP** | dominant; K=3456, tensor-core GEMM |\n| conv3 | ~109 GFLOP  | K=3456, tensor-core GEMM |\n| linear| ~32 GFLOP   | M=4096,K=3840,N=1024 GEMM |\n\n**Compute is dominated by conv2, then conv3.** Ideal conv2 ≈ 1.4 ms; realistic 2–4 ms.\n\n**Memory**: intermediate1 `[b,384,40,T1]` bf16 is *large*: w3 = 16·384·40·1024·2 ≈ **503 MB**\n(largest across wls; w1 400 MB, w5 452 MB). conv2 re-reads it (9 taps, with L2 reuse ≈ once).\nintermediate2 `[b,384,20,T2]` ≈ 126 MB (w3); intermediate3 ≈ 32 MB. So conv1 write +\nconv2 read of intermediate1 is the main bandwidth term (~0.5–1 GB traffic).\n\n### Why beating the reference is plausible\nThe reference launches ~10+ kernels: 3×(conv + gelu) = 6, a `permute→contiguous` copy (~1),\nlinear, `mul`, `add`. Our fused design uses **4 kernels** and removes the 32 MB\npermute-contiguous copy + several elementwise passes. cuDNN also handles the awkward\n**tiny freq dim** (Hout=20/10) which can push it to suboptimal algos/tiles; a purpose-built\nimplicit-GEMM with GELU/scale/PE fused into epilogues has real headroom even if per-conv we\nonly match cuDNN.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Triton design space\n\n### 3.1 Chosen pipeline (baseline candidate c001): 4 kernels, implicit-GEMM convs\n\n- **K-A `conv1+gelu`**: input `[b,1,80,Tin]` → intermediate1 stored **NHWC** `[b,40,T1,384]`.\n  Cin=1 so K=9; direct kernel: each program computes a tile of output pixels × BLOCK_N out\n  channels, gathers the 3×3 single-channel window (masked for pad), MAC over 9 taps\n  (pad K→16 for a `tl.dot`, or explicit FMA since Cout vectorizes well). Add bias, GELU,\n  round bf16.\n- **K-B `conv2+gelu`**: reads intermediate1 NHWC → intermediate2 NHWC `[b,20,T2,384]`.\n  Implicit GEMM: M=b·20·T2 output pixels, N=Cout=384, K=Cin·9=3456. Loop over the 9 taps;\n  each tap = `tl.dot(A[BLOCK_M, 384], W_tap[384, BLOCK_N])` fp32-accum. NHWC makes the A-block\n  read **contiguous along Cin** (coalesced). Bias + GELU + bf16 in epilogue.\n- **K-C `conv3+gelu` (fused reshape)**: reads intermediate2 NHWC → writes **directly into the\n  linear A-matrix** `[b, T3, 3840]` at column `cout*10 + f` (channel-outer/freq-inner). This\n  *is* the reference `permute(0,3,1,2).view(...)`, so the permute-contiguous copy disappears.\n  Same implicit-GEMM structure as K-B (M=b·10·T3).\n- **K-D `linear + scale + PE`**: GEMM `A[M=b·T3, K=3840] @ Wt[3840,1024]` with epilogue\n  `acc*embed_scale + positional_embedding[t]`, store bf16. A rows are contiguous in K\n  (coalesced); PE row selected by time index `t` (same across batch).\n\n**Layout decision**: store all intermediates **NHWC** (channels-last, contiguous C) so every\nimplicit-GEMM A-block load is coalesced. We *own* the intermediate buffers, so we pick layout\nfreely. The harness input is NCHW but Cin=1, so layout is irrelevant for conv1's input.\n\n**Weight layout for `tl.dot`**: convs need `W_tap[Cin, Cout]` = `weight[cout, cin, kh, kw]`\n(strided in the given `[Cout,Cin,3,3]`). Weight is tiny (2.65 MB, L2-resident and reused).\n- Option A (pure Triton, preferred for correctness-first): load with computed strides inside\n  the kernel — no torch compute, just strided global loads.\n- Option B (later opt): pre-permute weights to `[9,Cin,Cout]` contiguous. A `.permute().\n  contiguous()` is a **layout-only** transform (not the conv math) — arguably \"launch\n  plumbing,\" but it borders the \"no torch computation\" rule, so if used it will be done via a\n  small Triton copy kernel to stay unambiguous.\n\n### 3.2 Alternatives / later candidates\n- **im2col + cuBLAS/`tl.dot` GEMM**: rejected — materialized im2col for conv2 is ~0.9 GB\n  (M·3456), worse than implicit gather.\n- **Fuse conv1 into conv2** (skip 0.5 GB intermediate1): conv2 pixel needs a 3×3 window of\n  conv1 outputs, each conv1 output a 3×3 input window → up to 9× recompute of conv1. conv1 is\n  cheap (K=9), so trading 0.5 GB traffic for recompute may win. Candidate for a later variant.\n- **Fuse conv3 into linear**: linear row needs *all* 3840 K = all (c,f) of that time column of\n  conv3 → would recompute conv3 fully per N-tile; likely not worth it. Keep separate.\n- **Tap-blocking & tiling**: tune BLOCK_M∈{64,128}, BLOCK_N∈{64,128}, num_warps∈{4,8},\n  num_stages∈{2,3,4}. Small freq means M is dominated by time; tile M over flattened\n  (b,ho,wo). Boundary/pad handled by masking hi,wi at tap load.\n- **conv1 vectorization**: since it's memory-bound, may fuse gelu and prefer a plain\n  elementwise/tiled kernel over a padded `tl.dot`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical risks & mitigations\n\n- **Match rounding sites**: reference rounds each conv output to bf16 *before* GELU and each\n  intermediate to bf16. Mitigation: accumulate conv in **fp32**, round the pre-activation to\n  bf16, compute GELU in fp32 from that bf16 value, round result to bf16 → mirrors the ref's\n  bf16 intermediates and limits chained-error drift across the 3-conv cascade.\n- **GELU flavor**: use **exact erf** (`tl.math.erf`/libdevice), not tanh approx, to match\n  `F.gelu` default. tanh approx is likely within atol but erf is free-ish and safer.\n- **Accumulation precision**: fp32 accumulators for all `tl.dot` (Ampere bf16 MMA accum is\n  fp32 by default). Never accumulate in bf16.\n- **embed_scale**: apply as fp32 (32.0). PE added as fp32 then rounded to bf16. Output scaled\n  by 32 ⇒ absolute errors scale up, but `atol=1.1` (w3 1.3) already accounts for this.\n- **Padding correctness**: stride-2, pad-1 → boundary output pixels read out-of-range hi/wi;\n  mask to 0 (not the bias). Off-by-one here is the most likely correctness bug — assert Hout\n  formula `⌊(Hin−1)/2⌋+1` in the wrapper and cover freq edges (Hout row 0 and last).\n- **Flatten order**: `k = c*10 + f` (channel-outer). A swapped order silently passes shapes\n  but fails values — pin this in K-C's write indexing and cross-check against the weight’s\n  column semantics.\n- **bf16 dynamic range**: activations after Kaiming/Xavier init are O(1); no overflow risk.\n- **Tolerance headroom**: rtol 0.05, match_ratio 0.98 allow 2% outliers — comfortable for\n  bf16, but the 3-conv chain compounds rounding; if a workload fails, first suspect\n  rounding-site mismatch or pad masking, not raw precision.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Validation strategy\n\n- **No external harness allowed**: cannot run torch reference, CUDA, profiler, `nvidia-smi`,\n  or the evaluator directly. Correctness/speed come **only** from\n  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <cid>` over the 5 fixed workloads (= 1 evaluation).\n- **In-code guards** (cheap, allowed): shape/stride asserts in the Python wrapper; assert\n  computed T1/T2/T3 equal `⌊(n−1)/2⌋+1`; assert output shape `[b,T3,1024]` bf16; assert\n  `conv_out_dim=3840`, `d_model=1024`.\n- **Correctness-first ordering**: c001 = simplest fully-Triton correct pipeline (in-kernel\n  strided weight loads, modest fixed tiles, exact erf, bf16 rounding at ref sites). Only after\n  it passes all 5 do we tune tiles / fuse conv1→conv2 / weight pre-pack, each as a new\n  immutable candidate ID with its own evaluation.\n- **Regression discipline**: record per-workload pass + latency + geomean in `candidates.jsonl`;\n  keep changes small per candidate so a geomean regression is attributable.\n- **Fallback rule**: a failing Triton kernel is invalid — fix the Triton, never substitute a\n  torch/CPU/numpy path.\n\n### Success criteria\nGeomean speedup > 1.0 vs reference with **all 5 workloads passing** correctness; iterate on\nconv2/conv3 tiling (dominant cost) and the conv1→conv2 fusion for the memory-bound term.\n","numLines":177,"startLine":1,"totalLines":177}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Su2UCuyuvaZJM18tZP9RpX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"015_audio_sinusoidal_position_embedding_with_conv_projection\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Audio encoder's sinusoidal position embedding generation followed by 3-stage Conv2d downsampling (8x total reduction), GELU activations, and linear projection to model dimension. Processes mel-spectrogram through conv layers, adds cached sinusoidal position embeddings, and returns padded output.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_size\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Number of audio samples in batch\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"time_dim\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n12\t            \"description\": \"Time dimension of mel-spectrogram input\"\n13\t        },\n14\t        \"time_after_conv\": {\n15\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n16\t            \"description\": \"Time dimension after 3 stride-2 convolutions (approximately time_dim // 8)\"\n17\t        },\n18\t        \"d_model\": {\n19\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n20\t            \"value\": 1024,\n21\t            \"description\": \"Model hidden dimension\"\n22\t        },\n23\t        \"num_mel_bins\": {\n24\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n25\t            \"value\": 80,\n26\t            \"description\": \"Number of mel frequency bins\"\n27\t        },\n28\t        \"max_source_positions\": {\n29\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n30\t            \"value\": 1500,\n31\t            \"description\": \"Maximum sequence length for position embeddings\"\n32\t        },\n33\t        \"downsample_hidden_size\": {\n34\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n35\t            \"value\": 384,\n36\t            \"description\": \"Hidden size in conv layers\"\n37\t        },\n38\t        \"freq_dim_after_conv\": {\n39\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n40\t            \"value\": 10,\n41\t            \"description\": \"Frequency dimension after 3 stride-2 convs: 80->40->20->10\"\n42\t        },\n43\t        \"conv_out_dim\": {\n44\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n45\t            \"value\": 3840,\n46\t            \"description\": \"Flattened conv output: 384 * 10\"\n47\t        },\n48\t        \"one\": {\n49\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n50\t            \"value\": 1,\n51\t            \"description\": \"Constant 1 for input channel dimension\"\n52\t        },\n53\t        \"kernel_size\": {\n54\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n55\t            \"value\": 3,\n56\t            \"description\": \"Conv kernel size\"\n57\t        }\n58\t    },\n59\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": \"get_inputs\",\n60\t    \"inputs\": {\n61\t        \"input_features\": {\n62\t            \"shape\": [\n63\t                \"batch_size\",\n64\t                \"one\",\n65\t                \"num_mel_bins\",\n66\t                \"time_dim\"\n67\t            ],\n68\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n69\t            \"description\": \"Mel-spectrogram input\"\n70\t        },\n71\t        \"conv2d1_weight\": {\n72\t            \"shape\": [\n73\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\",\n74\t                \"one\",\n75\t                \"kernel_size\",\n76\t                \"kernel_size\"\n77\t            ],\n78\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n79\t            \"description\": \"First conv layer weights\"\n80\t        },\n81\t        \"conv2d1_bias\": {\n82\t            \"shape\": [\n83\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\"\n84\t            ],\n85\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n86\t            \"description\": \"First conv layer bias\"\n87\t        },\n88\t        \"conv2d2_weight\": {\n89\t            \"shape\": [\n90\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\",\n91\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\",\n92\t                \"kernel_size\",\n93\t                \"kernel_size\"\n94\t            ],\n95\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n96\t            \"description\": \"Second conv layer weights\"\n97\t        },\n98\t        \"conv2d2_bias\": {\n99\t            \"shape\": [\n100\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\"\n101\t            ],\n102\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n103\t            \"description\": \"Second conv layer bias\"\n104\t        },\n105\t        \"conv2d3_weight\": {\n106\t            \"shape\": [\n107\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\",\n108\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\",\n109\t                \"kernel_size\",\n110\t                \"kernel_size\"\n111\t            ],\n112\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n113\t            \"description\": \"Third conv layer weights\"\n114\t        },\n115\t        \"conv2d3_bias\": {\n116\t            \"shape\": [\n117\t                \"downsample_hidden_size\"\n118\t            ],\n119\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n120\t            \"description\": \"Third conv layer bias\"\n121\t        },\n122\t        \"conv_out_weight\": {\n123\t            \"shape\": [\n124\t                \"d_model\",\n125\t                \"conv_out_dim\"\n126\t            ],\n127\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n128\t            \"description\": \"Linear projection weights\"\n129\t        },\n130\t        \"positional_embedding\": {\n131\t            \"shape\": [\n132\t                \"max_source_positions\",\n133\t                \"d_model\"\n134\t            ],\n135\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n136\t            \"description\": \"Cached sinusoidal position embeddings\"\n137\t        },\n138\t        \"embed_scale\": {\n139\t            \"shape\": null,\n140\t            \"dtype\": \"float32\",\n141\t            \"description\": \"Embedding scale factor sqrt(d_model)\"\n142\t        }\n143\t    },\n144\t    \"outputs\": {\n145\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n146\t            \"shape\": [\n147\t                \"batch_size\",\n148\t                \"time_after_conv\",\n149\t                \"d_model\"\n150\t            ],\n151\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n152\t            \"description\": \"Output embeddings with position encoding added\"\n153\t        }\n154\t    },\n155\t    \"reference\": \"import math\\n\\nimport torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\ndef get_inputs(axes_and_scalars: dict, device: torch.device) -> dict[str, torch.Tensor]:\\n    batch_size = axes_and_scalars[\\\"batch_size\\\"]\\n    time_dim = axes_and_scalars[\\\"time_dim\\\"]\\n    d_model = 1024\\n    max_source_positions = 1500\\n    downsample_hidden_size = 384\\n    conv_out_dim = 3840  # 384 * 10\\n    kernel_size = 3\\n    dtype = torch.bfloat16\\n\\n    g = torch.Generator(device=device)\\n    g.manual_seed(42)\\n\\n    def kaiming_conv(out_c, in_c, kh, kw):\\n        fan_in = in_c * kh * kw\\n        return (torch.randn(out_c, in_c, kh, kw, device=device, generator=g) * math.sqrt(2.0 / fan_in)).to(dtype)\\n\\n    def xavier(out_f, in_f):\\n        return (torch.randn(out_f, in_f, device=device, generator=g) / math.sqrt(in_f)).to(dtype)\\n\\n    # Sinusoidal positional embedding\\n    pe = torch.zeros(max_source_positions, d_model, device=device)\\n    position = torch.arange(0, max_source_positions, device=device).unsqueeze(1).float()\\n    div_term = torch.exp(torch.arange(0, d_model, 2, device=device).float() * -(math.log(10000.0) / d_model))\\n    pe[:, 0::2] = torch.sin(position * div_term)\\n    pe[:, 1::2] = torch.cos(position * div_term)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"input_features\\\": torch.randn(batch_size, 1, 80, time_dim, device=device, generator=g).to(dtype),\\n        # Conv weights \\u2014 Kaiming init\\n        \\\"conv2d1_weight\\\": kaiming_conv(downsample_hidden_size, 1, kernel_size, kernel_size),\\n        \\\"conv2d1_bias\\\": torch.randn(downsample_hidden_size, device=device, generator=g).to(dtype),\\n        \\\"conv2d2_weight\\\": kaiming_conv(downsample_hidden_size, downsample_hidden_size, kernel_size, kernel_size),\\n        \\\"conv2d2_bias\\\": torch.randn(downsample_hidden_size, device=device, generator=g).to(dtype),\\n        \\\"conv2d3_weight\\\": kaiming_conv(downsample_hidden_size, downsample_hidden_size, kernel_size, kernel_size),\\n        \\\"conv2d3_bias\\\": torch.randn(downsample_hidden_size, device=device, generator=g).to(dtype),\\n        # Linear projection weight\\n        \\\"conv_out_weight\\\": xavier(d_model, conv_out_dim),\\n        # Sinusoidal positional embedding\\n        \\\"positional_embedding\\\": pe.to(dtype),\\n        # embed_scale = sqrt(d_model)\\n        \\\"embed_scale\\\": math.sqrt(d_model),\\n    }\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    input_features: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d1_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d1_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d2_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d2_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d3_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv2d3_bias: torch.Tensor,\\n    conv_out_weight: torch.Tensor,\\n    positional_embedding: torch.Tensor,\\n    embed_scale: float,\\n):\\n    # Stage 1: Conv2d (1 -> 384 channels) + GELU\\n    x = F.conv2d(input_features, conv2d1_weight, conv2d1_bias, stride=2, padding=1)\\n    x = F.gelu(x)\\n    \\n    # Stage 2: Conv2d (384 -> 384 channels) + GELU\\n    x = F.conv2d(x, conv2d2_weight, conv2d2_bias, stride=2, padding=1)\\n    x = F.gelu(x)\\n    \\n    # Stage 3: Conv2d (384 -> 384 channels) + GELU\\n    x = F.conv2d(x, conv2d3_weight, conv2d3_bias, stride=2, padding=1)\\n    x = F.gelu(x)\\n    \\n    # Reshape: (batch, channels, freq, time) -> (batch, time, channels*freq)\\n    b, c, f, t = x.size()\\n    x = x.permute(0, 3, 1, 2).contiguous().view(b, t, c * f)\\n    \\n    # Linear projection to d_model (no bias)\\n    x = F.linear(x, conv_out_weight)\\n    \\n    # Scale embeddings\\n    x = x * embed_scale\\n    \\n    # Add positional embeddings\\n    seq_len = x.shape[1]\\n    pos_embed = positional_embedding[:seq_len, :].unsqueeze(0)\\n    x = x + pos_embed\\n    \\n    return x\\n\"\n156\t}\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef68dcaa-cfdb-437b-a141-fe330f865751","uuid":"c0cc7d71-be2f-43b3-b32e-b99531b7ccd8","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:57:22.065Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-015_audio_sinusoidal_position_embedding_with_conv_projection/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"015_audio_sinusoidal_position_embedding_with_conv_projection\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"Qwen/Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct\",\n    \"description\": \"Audio encoder's sinusoidal position embedding generation followed by 3-stage Conv2d downsampling (8x total reduction), GELU activations, and linear projection to model dimension. Processes mel-spectrogram through conv layers, adds cached sinusoidal position embeddings, and returns padded output.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Number of audio samples in batch\"\n        },\n        \"time_dim\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Time dimension of mel-spectrogram input\"\n        },\n        \"time_after_conv\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Time dimension after 3 stride-2 convolutions (approximately time_dim // 8)\"\n        },\n        \"d_model\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 1024,\n            \"description\": \"Model hidden dimension\"\n        },\n        \"num_mel_bins\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 80,\n            \"description\": \"Number of mel frequency bins\"\n        },\n        \"max_source_positions\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 1500,\n            \"description\": \"Maximum sequence length for position embeddings\"\n        },\n        \"downsample_hidden_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 384,\n            \"description\